The president is on a tight schedule.

201
00:14:58,022 --> 00:14:59,023
Excuse me.

202
00:15:16,123 --> 00:15:18,000
Well, this is a surprise.

203
00:15:19,627 --> 00:15:22,002
You know how Midwesterners
feel about those.

204
00:15:22,004 --> 00:15:25,589
Oh, don't listen to him.
It's about time you came out.

205
00:15:25,591 --> 00:15:27,810
Seems we have some things to discuss.

206
00:15:27,813 --> 00:15:28,967
Yeah.

207
00:15:28,969 --> 00:15:32,304
Well, as far as this special
election is concerned, Claire,

208
00:15:32,306 --> 00:15:36,208
you need to know that the
soon-to-be-retiring Miss Gallagher

209
00:15:36,211 --> 00:15:38,143
is out of question.

210
00:15:38,145 --> 00:15:42,731
And I'm happy to see we're both fans
of the Declaration of Independence,

211
00:15:42,733 --> 00:15:44,199
unalienable rights and all that.

212
00:15:44,202 --> 00:15:46,777
But it says down here in the
speech you're gonna give later...

213
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:48,615
Miss Gallagher has my support.

214
00:15:50,824 --> 00:15:53,075
I believe Mark conveyed our concerns.

215
00:15:53,077 --> 00:15:54,828
And communicated mine, I hope.

216
00:15:55,579 --> 00:15:58,747
She's a tax and spend Democrat
underneath that uniform.

217
00:15:58,749 --> 00:16:01,374
- A patriot with a pristine resume.
- Claire.

218
00:16:01,377 --> 00:16:04,378
Who is very vocal about
keeping private contractors away

219
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:06,797
- from US military operations.
- Even as a negotiating point,

220
00:16:06,799 --> 00:16:07,923
you can do better than her.

221
00:16:07,925 --> 00:16:10,175
And I am tired of these
delays, Madam President.

222
00:16:10,177 --> 00:16:11,702
It is time for you to deliver.

223
00:16:15,015 --> 00:16:17,641
- Your husband and I had an agreement.
- Francis is dead.

224
00:16:17,643 --> 00:16:19,937
- Promises were made.
- Not by me.

225
00:16:22,147 --> 00:16:26,334
Just like you, I like to
negotiate my own agreements.

226
00:16:26,337 --> 00:16:29,695
And make no mistake about it, the
lieutenant general is not an anomaly.

227
00:16:29,697 --> 00:16:32,823
I know that. I've seen your list
of candidates for the midterms...

228
00:16:32,825 --> 00:16:35,993
- Yes, mostly women.
- I don't care what sex they are.

229
00:16:35,995 --> 00:16:38,704
And for the record, nobody
loves women more than me.

230
00:16:38,706 --> 00:16:41,164
But this slate of yours, it
will bankrupt the country.

231
00:16:41,166 --> 00:16:44,126
They will set us back a
generation. They will completely...

232
00:16:44,128 --> 00:16:47,491
Support me and what I stand for.

233
00:16:47,494 --> 00:16:48,672
And what is that?

234
00:16:48,674 --> 00:16:50,424
- Oh, come on, Bill.
- No, no. Tell me.

235
00:16:50,426 --> 00:16:52,092
I wanna know. What's your mission?

236
00:16:52,094 --> 00:16:54,344
- I wanna know what your platform is.
- Oh, please.

237
00:16:54,346 --> 00:16:56,638
I know you'd like to
drown DC all at once.

238
00:16:56,640 --> 00:16:59,182
But I'm gonna tell you right
now, I cannot let that happen.

239
00:16:59,184 --> 00:17:01,643
I still believe in
government and its ability

240
00:17:01,645 --> 00:17:04,350
to make the everyday lives
of its citizens better.

241
00:17:04,353 --> 00:17:06,395
- Yeah.
- You don't wanna support my slate,

242
00:17:06,398 --> 00:17:07,443
that's fine.

243
00:17:08,193 --> 00:17:11,155
- Claire...
- All I ask is stay out of the way.

244
00:17:14,491 --> 00:17:16,535
There's a bill sitting on your desk.

245
00:17:18,162 --> 00:17:20,579
A bill that took years...

246
00:17:20,581 --> 00:17:23,457
and money to get passed.

247
00:17:23,459 --> 00:17:24,793
You sign that,

248
00:17:25,544 --> 00:17:27,838
and I know I got a
friend in the White House.

249
00:17:29,381 --> 00:17:30,549
If not...

250
00:17:32,301 --> 00:17:33,886
- Then I don't.
- Enough.

251
00:17:37,056 --> 00:17:39,473
Nothing good happens to
you when she's around.

252
00:17:39,475 --> 00:17:40,934
Nothing.

253
00:17:40,937 --> 00:17:43,353
Why did you come out for Francis?

254
00:17:43,356 --> 00:17:45,075
For his foundation?

255
00:17:45,078 --> 00:17:47,121
I don't think you ever really liked him.

256
00:17:48,317 --> 00:17:51,695
Probably never respected
him. Why back him now?

257
00:17:53,572 --> 00:17:55,199
Because he gave us you.

258
00:17:58,911 --> 00:18:01,747
Tell our friends I'll be back
down in time for the fireworks.

259
00:18:10,297 --> 00:18:13,342
Just so you know, I always liked Frank.

260
00:18:15,677 --> 00:18:17,137
His desperation.

261
00:18:21,266 --> 00:18:23,060
I'm sorry you can't stay.

262
00:18:23,685 --> 00:18:26,146
I do enjoy curating our little show.

263
00:18:27,356 --> 00:18:30,857
I collect a variety of
fireworks shells as I travel.

264
00:18:30,859 --> 00:18:32,653
Italy, Spain, China.

265
00:18:34,446 --> 00:18:36,949
Do you know what the
hardest color to make is?

266
00:18:39,201 --> 00:18:41,743
- Red?
- No, red's easy.

267
00:18:41,745 --> 00:18:42,955
White's easy.

268
00:18:44,123 --> 00:18:45,580
It's blue.

269
00:18:45,582 --> 00:18:48,834
Very tricky chemistry
involving copper and heat.

270
00:18:48,836 --> 00:18:51,463
A truly bright blue
is extremely elusive.

271
00:18:53,757 --> 00:18:56,468
Whatever Francis promised or did...

272
00:18:57,594 --> 00:18:59,054
is buried with him.

273
00:18:59,930 --> 00:19:01,223
No, I know.

274
00:19:02,891 --> 00:19:04,977
We all need to let him go.

275
00:19:05,894 --> 00:19:08,230
Start from some kind of scratch.

276
00:19:10,816 --> 00:19:12,276
Though it must haunt you,

277
00:19:13,652 --> 00:19:15,821
the fact that he died
next to you in bed.

278
00:19:23,300 --> 00:19:24,496
Hon...

279
00:19:27,207 --> 00:19:28,250
be patient.

280
00:19:33,025 --> 00:19:35,922
- She doesn't like the name?
- I think she finds it incomplete.

281
00:19:35,924 --> 00:19:37,591
What's wrong with the
Comprehensive Safety Act?

282
00:19:37,593 --> 00:19:39,134
Comprehensive Chemical Safety Act?

283
00:19:39,136 --> 00:19:41,052
Or we could go back to
the Smarter Safety Act.

284
00:19:41,054 --> 00:19:44,139
- Would she like "Smarter" better?
- No, we need a good acronym.

285
00:19:44,141 --> 00:19:46,425
Mr. Vice President? Mark?

286
00:19:48,729 --> 00:19:49,996
Yeah. Agreed.

287
00:19:49,999 --> 00:19:53,982
At one point, we talked about
the Repeal Unsafe Regulation Act.

288
00:19:53,984 --> 00:19:55,817
That's RURAL if we come up with an L.

289
00:19:55,819 --> 00:19:56,818
Legislation?

290
00:19:56,820 --> 00:19:58,486
That doesn't make sense
with the rest of it.

291
00:19:58,488 --> 00:20:00,822
"RURAL" is too hard
to say. "SAFE" is good.

292
00:20:00,824 --> 00:20:02,490
Safety. Future.

293
00:20:02,492 --> 00:20:07,120
- I like "FUTURE."
- Federal Universal Toxic and Unsafe...

294
00:20:07,122 --> 00:20:09,041
- Regulation Eradication.
- Okay.

295
00:20:09,666 --> 00:20:12,375
- The FUTURE Act.
- That could work.

296
00:20:12,377 --> 00:20:16,632
May I ask, Mr. Vice President, does
her hesitation go beyond the name?

297
00:20:18,300 --> 00:20:20,427
She'll come around. I'm guessing...

298
00:20:21,261 --> 00:20:23,220
by the end of the day.

299
00:20:23,222 --> 00:20:24,846
Because in one form or another,

300
00:20:24,848 --> 00:20:27,448
- this bill has been on her desk since...
- She'll sign it, Henry.

301
00:20:28,477 --> 00:20:30,062
Bill.

302
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:35,425
What do you want me to do?

303
00:20:35,428 --> 00:20:37,388
Yank her from her own military base?

304
00:20:41,490 --> 00:20:45,784
When, in the course of human
events, it becomes necessary

305
00:20:45,786 --> 00:20:48,417
for one people to dissolve
the political bands

306
00:20:48,420 --> 00:20:50,704
which have connected them with another,

307
00:20:50,707 --> 00:20:52,542
you just have to do it.

308
00:20:53,252 --> 00:20:55,003
Sever the relationship.

309
00:20:56,088 --> 00:20:58,421
Our nation's founding document,

310
00:20:58,423 --> 00:21:00,507
the Declaration of Independence...

311
00:21:00,509 --> 00:21:02,008
America's independence...

312
00:21:02,010 --> 00:21:04,721
Was hard-fought and hard-won.

313
00:21:05,430 --> 00:21:06,638
Are you still there?

314
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:10,727
Our Founding Fathers imagined
a nation that was free.

315
00:21:11,436 --> 00:21:13,520
Free from despotism.

316
00:21:13,522 --> 00:21:16,106
Free from absolute tyranny.

317
00:21:16,108 --> 00:21:18,733
Free from an oppressive king.

318
00:21:18,735 --> 00:21:19,985
Do you miss Francis?

319
00:21:19,987 --> 00:21:21,989
Tyrants come in many forms.

320
00:21:23,073 --> 00:21:27,536
From the American oligarchs trying to
change the very fabric of our society,

321
00:21:28,537 --> 00:21:30,998
to autocrats threatening from abroad.

322
00:21:32,165 --> 00:21:36,795
Now, each one of us has
to defend our destiny.

323
00:21:37,838 --> 00:21:39,337
And as I see it,

324
00:21:39,339 --> 00:21:43,969
the fight against tyranny is
the only justification for war.

325
00:21:45,512 --> 00:21:49,683
My first 100 days as
president have been difficult.

326
00:21:51,018 --> 00:21:52,477
I lost my husband.

327
00:21:53,687 --> 00:21:56,315
We were about to
celebrate 30 years together.

328
00:21:57,316 --> 00:21:59,816
But I know what he would want.

329
00:21:59,818 --> 00:22:02,696
For me to stop mourning and carry on.

330
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:07,115
With the help of women like
Lieutenant General Nancy Gallagher,

331
00:22:07,117 --> 00:22:09,494
my presidency will not rest.

332
00:22:10,162 --> 00:22:12,954
Nancy has been an inspiration to me.

333
00:22:12,956 --> 00:22:14,831
A tireless warrior.

334
00:22:14,833 --> 00:22:18,877
I promise her and all of you that
I will work on behalf of the rights

335
00:22:18,879 --> 00:22:22,758
and freedoms earned over the
last 241 years of our democracy.

336
00:22:24,801 --> 00:22:26,634
As I look into the eyes

337
00:22:26,636 --> 00:22:29,429
of you brave soldiers
standing before me today...

338
00:22:29,431 --> 00:22:30,430
Here's the thing.

339
00:22:30,432 --> 00:22:33,016
I'm feeling so proud right now.

340
00:22:33,018 --> 00:22:35,226
Proud to be your Commander-in-Chief.

341
00:22:35,228 --> 00:22:37,896
Whatever Francis told
you the last five years,

342
00:22:37,898 --> 00:22:39,356
don't believe a word of it.

343
00:22:39,358 --> 00:22:42,569
Our country is 241
years old, my friends.

344
00:22:43,195 --> 00:22:45,737
Let's wish this great
nation a happy birthday.

345
00:22:45,739 --> 00:22:47,030
Say it with me.

346
00:22:47,032 --> 00:22:49,449
Happy birthday, America!

347
00:22:49,451 --> 00:22:52,110
Happy Birthday, America!

348
00:22:52,113 --> 00:22:53,828
It's going to be
different for you and me.

349
00:22:53,830 --> 00:22:54,996
One more time.

350
00:22:54,998 --> 00:22:56,373
I'm going to tell you the truth.

351
00:22:56,375 --> 00:22:58,835
Happy Birthday, America.

352
00:23:00,259 --> 00:23:01,899
- Thank you for your service.
- Thank you.

353
00:23:03,006 --> 00:23:04,466
Thank you for your service.

354
00:23:05,550 --> 00:23:07,469
- Thank you for your service.
- Thank you.

355
00:23:09,346 --> 00:23:12,430
I want you to know how wonderful
it is to be here and meet

356
00:23:12,432 --> 00:23:14,099
all of you new recruits.

357
00:23:14,101 --> 00:23:15,483
So, thank you.

358
00:23:15,486 --> 00:23:17,018
Thank you for your service.

359
00:23:17,020 --> 00:23:18,937
- Thank you.
- Thank you, ma'am.

360
00:23:18,939 --> 00:23:21,523
Thank you for your service.
Where are you from, private?

361
00:23:21,525 --> 00:23:23,026
Kalamazoo, Mr. President.

362
00:23:24,152 --> 00:23:26,653
- Excuse me, Madam President.
- That's quite all right.

363
00:23:26,655 --> 00:23:28,279
I actually hate "Madam."

364
00:23:28,281 --> 00:23:30,824
Makes me sound like I'm running
a brothel and not a country.

365
00:23:30,826 --> 00:23:33,493
- Thank you for your service.
- Thank you, Madam President.

366
00:23:33,495 --> 00:23:35,120
Thank you for your service.

367
00:23:35,122 --> 00:23:36,871
And where are you from, Pvt. Burton?

368
00:23:36,873 --> 00:23:38,206
South Bend, ma'am.

369
00:23:38,208 --> 00:23:40,500
Oh, I love Indiana.

370
00:23:40,502 --> 00:23:42,585
I enlisted right before you took office.

371
00:23:42,587 --> 00:23:45,588
Well, I promise you and
every woman in this country

372
00:23:45,590 --> 00:23:47,090
that during my presidency

373
00:23:47,092 --> 00:23:49,843
the key components of
the Equal Rights Amendment

374
00:23:49,845 --> 00:23:51,469
will finally pass.

375
00:23:51,471 --> 00:23:53,098
I'm going to make sure of it.

376
00:23:53,807 --> 00:23:55,600
I want you to know I support you.

377
00:23:56,452 --> 00:23:58,270
Do you even have a plan?

378
00:23:59,038 --> 00:24:00,230
What did you say?

379
00:24:01,643 --> 00:24:04,060
One that won't get us all killed?

380
00:24:04,063 --> 00:24:05,314
Oh, man...

381
00:24:06,778 --> 00:24:09,072
Would you have asked
me that if I were a man?

382
00:24:18,429 --> 00:24:19,791
Madam President!

383
00:24:21,126 --> 00:24:24,212
One question, please!

384
00:25:33,907 --> 00:25:37,075
How much longer till
we get to the plane?

385
00:25:37,077 --> 00:25:39,911
Just another 25 minutes
or so, Madam President.

386
00:25:39,913 --> 00:25:41,704
Okay, thank you.

387
00:25:41,706 --> 00:25:43,122
Shit!

388
00:25:44,459 --> 00:25:46,918
- Is she shot?
- Stay down, stay down!

389
00:25:46,920 --> 00:25:48,866
Shots fired! Shots fired!

390
00:25:48,869 --> 00:25:50,543
Are you okay, Madam President?

391
00:25:50,546 --> 00:25:51,893
- Are you all right?
- Yes.

392
00:25:52,952 --> 00:25:54,302
Lone Star is safe.

393
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:21,304
Sir, someone just tried
to kill the president.

394
00:26:23,039 --> 00:26:25,248
I just got the alert. Is she all right?

395
00:26:25,250 --> 00:26:26,452
This way.

396
00:26:42,517 --> 00:26:43,560
Lincoln,

397
00:26:45,061 --> 00:26:48,481
Garfield, McKinley, Kennedy.

398
00:26:49,816 --> 00:26:52,193
Successful assassinations,
total of four.

399
00:26:56,239 --> 00:26:58,908
The attempts... Nine?

400
00:27:00,243 --> 00:27:03,246
Jackson, both Roosevelts.

401
00:27:04,706 --> 00:27:06,750
Truman. Ford twice.

402
00:27:07,459 --> 00:27:08,501
Reagan...

403
00:27:09,856 --> 00:27:11,942
the first Underwood and...

404
00:27:14,507 --> 00:27:17,510
I will say, whoever tried to kill me,

405
00:27:18,303 --> 00:27:23,099
perversely, it's the first sign of
real respect I've gotten in 100 days.

406
00:27:33,026 --> 00:27:34,444
Somebody tried to kill her.

407
00:27:36,488 --> 00:27:37,745
Bill.

408
00:27:40,183 --> 00:27:41,493
Can you blame them?

409
00:27:43,703 --> 00:27:45,955
The Underwoods have always
been hard of hearing.

410
00:27:47,040 --> 00:27:48,831
She's not him.

411
00:27:48,833 --> 00:27:50,333
No, no.

412
00:27:50,335 --> 00:27:51,876
She is not.

413
00:27:51,878 --> 00:27:53,963
You know that better than anyone.

414
00:27:56,341 --> 00:27:57,634
Is it public?

415
00:27:58,385 --> 00:28:01,754
Secret Service shut it down.
Standard procedure, apparently.

416
00:28:01,757 --> 00:28:03,930
Threats beget threats.

417
00:28:05,433 --> 00:28:07,148
Bill...

418
00:28:07,151 --> 00:28:08,853
were you behind this?

419
00:28:12,399 --> 00:28:13,650
We're gonna miss the show.

420
00:28:22,367 --> 00:28:24,531
- How are y'all?
- Madam President.

421
00:28:24,534 --> 00:28:25,574
Hi, I'm so sorry.

422
00:28:25,577 --> 00:28:27,661
I want to apologize
for the late departure.

423
00:28:27,664 --> 00:28:30,748
I know you all have families
and festivities waiting.

424
00:28:30,750 --> 00:28:34,377
Madam President, may
I ask what happened?

425
00:28:34,379 --> 00:28:39,549
Oh, the Beast was re-routed due to
security concerns. Pretty routine.

426
00:28:39,551 --> 00:28:43,046
I know it's been a while
since I've been back here.

427
00:28:43,049 --> 00:28:45,820
I just wanted to wish you
all a very happy Fourth,

428
00:28:45,823 --> 00:28:49,684
even if you're gonna be celebrating
it on the fifth. I'm so sorry.

429
00:28:49,686 --> 00:28:52,140
Your speech, afterwards with...

430
00:28:52,143 --> 00:28:53,894
Yes, the new recruit.

431
00:28:54,933 --> 00:28:58,560
If a new recruit, especially a
female one, can't speak her mind,

432
00:28:58,570 --> 00:29:01,781
I don't know what kind of
country we actually are.

433
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:08,035
And be sure to tell us
if you run out of beer.

434
00:29:08,037 --> 00:29:10,329
Because I am sure there's
a couple of cases left over

435
00:29:10,331 --> 00:29:12,876
from the Carter administration.

436
00:29:13,710 --> 00:29:15,077
And you're new.

437
00:29:16,211 --> 00:29:19,663
Yes, ma'am. Melody Cruz.
I'm with Beltway Television.

438
00:29:19,666 --> 00:29:22,208
Well, I hope everyone is
making you feel at home.

439
00:29:22,211 --> 00:29:23,342
Very much so.

440
00:29:23,344 --> 00:29:25,636
It's an honor to finally meet you.

441
00:29:25,638 --> 00:29:27,054
I'm a bit overwhelmed, sorry.

442
00:29:27,056 --> 00:29:29,726
- Don't worry, I don't bite.
- No, I know.

443
00:29:31,144 --> 00:29:34,773
It's strange, I was supposed
to interview your husband.

444
00:29:35,732 --> 00:29:37,984
It was scheduled for a
few days after he died.

445
00:29:39,152 --> 00:29:42,987
It was going to be an exclusive.
We'd had a number of pre-interviews.

446
00:29:42,989 --> 00:29:44,657
Questions we'd agreed on.

447
00:29:45,937 --> 00:29:47,285
Questions like?

448
00:29:48,870 --> 00:29:51,790
Like, was the president
going to pardon him?

449
00:29:52,707 --> 00:29:53,750
And when?

450
00:29:54,501 --> 00:29:56,753
Will you? Even now, posthumously?

451
00:29:59,925 --> 00:30:03,093
I know you did an exclusive
with The New York Examiner,

452
00:30:03,096 --> 00:30:06,632
butl'm guessing that's only
going to scratch the surface.

453
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:09,015
I bet you have more to say

454
00:30:09,773 --> 00:30:12,099
- about the state of your marriage...
- Miss...

455
00:30:12,101 --> 00:30:13,392
Cruz, is it?

456
00:30:13,394 --> 00:30:15,102
I don't know if anyone's told you,

457
00:30:15,104 --> 00:30:17,230
but when the president
visits this part of the plane,

458
00:30:17,232 --> 00:30:19,192
it's usually considered off the record.

459
00:30:19,195 --> 00:30:21,484
But you can ask my press
secretary anything you like

460
00:30:21,486 --> 00:30:22,804
once we're back on the ground.

461
00:30:33,581 --> 00:30:35,500
You're especially quiet this evening.

462
00:30:37,126 --> 00:30:38,711
Maybe I'm a little agitated.

463
00:30:43,591 --> 00:30:46,926
Our patients don't do too well
when it comes to fireworks.

464
00:30:46,928 --> 00:30:49,303
Ah. Pardon me.

465
00:30:56,145 --> 00:30:57,478
Yes?

466
00:30:57,480 --> 00:31:00,108
I don't understand why you
leave that on when we're meeting.

467
00:31:00,817 --> 00:31:04,694
Well, it's not like you're
saying anything different or new.

468
00:31:04,696 --> 00:31:07,196
Your dedication to your
script would be admirable

469
00:31:07,198 --> 00:31:09,033
if it weren't so concerning.

470
00:31:12,412 --> 00:31:15,121
In a few weeks, I'll be
appearing at a hearing

471
00:31:15,123 --> 00:31:17,166
regarding your state of mind

472
00:31:18,251 --> 00:31:20,751
and when it comes to your confession,

473
00:31:20,753 --> 00:31:23,715
even a guilty man changes
his story now and then.

474
00:31:27,886 --> 00:31:29,220
Who do you answer to?

475
00:31:30,597 --> 00:31:32,015
I mean, who do you work for?

476
00:31:33,683 --> 00:31:35,310
You.

477
00:31:37,136 --> 00:31:38,846
That's the idea, anyway.

478
00:31:40,398 --> 00:31:42,191
Everybody works for somebody.

479
00:31:48,573 --> 00:31:50,950
I worked for a man who refused to fail.

480
00:31:53,202 --> 00:31:54,662
I respected that.

481
00:31:55,830 --> 00:31:57,206
I appreciated it.

482
00:32:02,795 --> 00:32:04,526
I'd be dead without him.

483
00:32:04,529 --> 00:32:06,049
Why?

484
00:32:17,393 --> 00:32:20,563
I was working as an
aide for a congressman.

485
00:32:21,356 --> 00:32:23,648
He was idealistic and naive.

486
00:32:23,650 --> 00:32:25,568
Had one-term written all over him.

487
00:32:27,236 --> 00:32:30,615
But I needed a job, a place to be.

488
00:32:32,200 --> 00:32:33,952
I was barely keeping it together.

489
00:32:37,956 --> 00:32:40,041
Francis and I met at some function.

490
00:32:40,951 --> 00:32:42,661
You know, the kind you stand up at.

491
00:32:46,506 --> 00:32:49,884
I don't know how he knew, but
I had eight bottles in my trunk.

492
00:32:51,052 --> 00:32:53,172
I got through four that
night when he came to my door.

493
00:32:55,682 --> 00:32:57,016
I don't remember much.

494
00:32:58,184 --> 00:32:59,352
Not clearly.

495
00:33:03,147 --> 00:33:04,399
He took me to his home.

496
00:33:06,192 --> 00:33:07,360
But Claire...

497
00:33:11,155 --> 00:33:12,615
Francis got me dry.

498
00:33:14,951 --> 00:33:17,412
I shook so badly I
thought I was gonna die.

499
00:33:20,123 --> 00:33:22,041
And then when I was finally got clean,

500
00:33:23,084 --> 00:33:24,752
he gave me my first job.

501
00:33:27,088 --> 00:33:28,131
As an aide?

502
00:33:30,133 --> 00:33:31,718
Hmm. Sort of.

503
00:33:33,386 --> 00:33:36,465
He asked me to go talk to
Congressman Kyle from Iowa.

504
00:33:36,468 --> 00:33:37,890
To what end?

505
00:33:40,018 --> 00:33:41,269
To persuade him.

506
00:33:44,105 --> 00:33:45,273
And I did.

507
00:33:51,404 --> 00:33:52,488
But Claire...

508
00:33:54,365 --> 00:33:55,950
The way she treated him,

509
00:33:58,369 --> 00:33:59,871
in life and in death...

510
00:34:08,588 --> 00:34:11,132
But you pay a price, when you
work for somebody like him.

511
00:34:12,216 --> 00:34:13,426
Somebody you owe.

512
00:34:22,310 --> 00:34:24,604
Somebody close to me
died in a car accident.

513
00:34:28,149 --> 00:34:30,234
That's how I broke the
terms of my house arrest.

514
00:34:33,488 --> 00:34:35,198
I went to see the site of her wreck.

515
00:34:37,075 --> 00:34:39,160
It was twisted on the side of the road.

516
00:34:43,372 --> 00:34:44,749
I just stood there.

517
00:34:46,459 --> 00:34:47,585
An accident?

518
00:34:50,588 --> 00:34:51,839
So they said.

519
00:34:54,550 --> 00:34:55,927
And Zoe Barnes?

520
00:34:59,263 --> 00:35:00,348
I killed her.

521
00:35:02,433 --> 00:35:03,726
For the president.

522
00:35:14,862 --> 00:35:16,572
That's what he used to do when he won.

523
00:35:20,409 --> 00:35:23,204
What did he leave you with?

524
00:35:30,920 --> 00:35:35,299
Last thing that Francis gave me
was the letter opener from his desk.

525
00:35:37,343 --> 00:35:38,845
I mean, I chose it, but...

526
00:35:42,974 --> 00:35:45,935
But I guess really the last
thing he gave me was an apology.

527
00:35:48,229 --> 00:35:50,106
I spoke to him the night he died.

528
00:37:22,740 --> 00:37:25,243
My presidency will not rest.

529
00:37:25,910 --> 00:37:29,787
Nancy has been an inspiration
to me. A tireless warrior.

530
00:37:29,789 --> 00:37:33,624
I promise her and all of you that
I will work on behalf of the rights

531
00:37:33,626 --> 00:37:37,672
and freedoms earned over the
last 241 years of our democracy.

532
00:37:38,547 --> 00:37:40,381
Tyrants come in many forms...

533
00:37:40,383 --> 00:37:41,340
Pathetic.

534
00:37:41,342 --> 00:37:44,011
From the American oligarchs
trying to change the very...

535
00:37:52,895 --> 00:37:55,980
Are you sure you don't want
to take an hour or two to...

536
00:37:55,982 --> 00:37:58,369
Mark. I'm inside the White House.

537
00:37:58,372 --> 00:38:00,932
There's only two of us in the
Oval, so unless you're packing,

538
00:38:00,935 --> 00:38:02,635
it's literally the
safest place I can be.

539
00:38:02,638 --> 00:38:04,571
Well, I'm not packing, Madam President,

540
00:38:04,573 --> 00:38:07,702
but no one would fault you for
taking a breath for a minute.

541
00:38:09,495 --> 00:38:10,955
I'm breathing just fine.

542
00:38:12,248 --> 00:38:14,208
Can we keep news of this contained?

543
00:38:14,917 --> 00:38:17,753
- I've taken every possible step, I think.
- So who is he?

544
00:38:19,213 --> 00:38:22,339
Well, they did forensics on the route.

545
00:38:22,341 --> 00:38:24,552
Compared notes with the aerial detail.

546
00:38:25,428 --> 00:38:28,055
Homeland Secretary and
the Bureau were brought in.

547
00:38:28,848 --> 00:38:32,101
He never left the place
he took the shot from.

548
00:38:37,648 --> 00:38:40,568
Can you answer a single
question directly and simply?

549
00:38:43,362 --> 00:38:47,992
He shot himself in the head with
his own US military-issued handgun.

550
00:38:50,202 --> 00:38:51,702
And we're sure he's the one?

551
00:38:51,704 --> 00:38:53,789
Yeah. Pro-militia. Anti-government.

552
00:38:54,498 --> 00:38:57,291
Dishonorably discharged last
year. All the typical markers.

553
00:38:57,293 --> 00:38:59,501
Secret Service is confident.

554
00:38:59,503 --> 00:39:01,088
And the Bureau's investigating.

555
00:39:04,383 --> 00:39:07,011
How did Bill Shepherd get
his hands on my speech?

556
00:39:08,429 --> 00:39:09,803
What? Um...

557
00:39:09,805 --> 00:39:13,057
Well, the press usually
gets it hours before.

558
00:39:13,059 --> 00:39:15,269
It wouldn't be that hard
to access a copy, I guess.

559
00:39:18,731 --> 00:39:20,274
I won't be signing that today.

560
00:39:25,446 --> 00:39:28,305
You went there to declare your
independence, and you declared war.

561
00:39:28,308 --> 00:39:29,406
That was a mistake.

562
00:39:29,408 --> 00:39:31,325
No, the mistake was I
didn't hire a chief of staff,

563
00:39:31,327 --> 00:39:33,077
and things are slipping
through the cracks.

564
00:39:33,079 --> 00:39:36,079
That's where I have to disagree with
you. We need to keep this circle small.

565
00:39:37,208 --> 00:39:39,460
You know, Francis warned me about you.

566
00:39:44,006 --> 00:39:47,299
I assure you, I serve at your
pleasure, Madam President.

567
00:39:47,301 --> 00:39:50,179
You need to stop meeting
in my office without me.

568
00:39:53,703 --> 00:39:54,934
Yes, ma'am.

569
00:40:03,943 --> 00:40:06,031
Secret Service took the lead.

570
00:40:06,034 --> 00:40:07,778
But do you believe the theory?

571
00:40:07,780 --> 00:40:11,782
That it was a disgruntled service
person who took a shot at me?

572
00:40:11,784 --> 00:40:12,952
PID does.

573
00:40:14,662 --> 00:40:16,163
I'm asking what you think.

574
00:40:18,541 --> 00:40:23,129
It depends if it was a
warning or an actual attempt.

575
00:40:24,255 --> 00:40:27,923
Given the accuracy, the shooter
would have been informed enough to know

576
00:40:27,925 --> 00:40:30,803
that a 50 caliber round
wouldn't penetrate the Beast.

577
00:40:32,346 --> 00:40:33,681
I think he wanted to scare you.

578
00:40:34,306 --> 00:40:35,848
On behalf of someone.

579
00:40:35,850 --> 00:40:38,475
Lots of people make threats
against sitting presidents.

580
00:40:38,477 --> 00:40:41,188
I'm starting to believe
Francis was murdered.

581
00:40:46,026 --> 00:40:49,572
Before he died, he aligned himself
with some very powerful people.

582
00:40:49,575 --> 00:40:51,904
And I think they turned on
him, and they wanted him dead,

583
00:40:51,907 --> 00:40:53,784
and I think they want me dead, too.

584
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:57,788
Do you think that sounds crazy?

585
00:40:59,915 --> 00:41:00,916
No, ma'am.

586
00:41:01,709 --> 00:41:04,209
But I think we need to bring
in Special Agent Bowman...

587
00:41:04,211 --> 00:41:06,587
Of these people, these
powerful families,

588
00:41:06,589 --> 00:41:10,299
they advocate dismantling
intelligence agencies.

589
00:41:10,301 --> 00:41:12,389
CIA, FBI, Homeland.

590
00:41:12,392 --> 00:41:13,594
You know that, right?

591
00:41:13,596 --> 00:41:15,429
- Ma'am...
- We cannot bring

592
00:41:15,431 --> 00:41:17,475
anyone else in at the moment.

593
00:41:19,143 --> 00:41:20,686
I'm relying on you

594
00:41:21,520 --> 00:41:23,397
the way I know my husband could.

595
00:41:24,023 --> 00:41:25,941
So, please see what you can find.

596
00:41:33,574 --> 00:41:35,240
Is it ever Nate

597
00:41:35,242 --> 00:41:36,410
or always Nathan?

598
00:41:37,886 --> 00:41:39,038
Nathan.

599
00:41:40,752 --> 00:41:42,252
Like Francis.

600
00:41:42,255 --> 00:41:43,542
For me, anyway.

601
00:41:48,797 --> 00:41:51,383
Oh, have you spoken to Doug Stamper?

602
00:41:54,162 --> 00:41:55,387
No.

603
00:41:57,348 --> 00:41:58,764
I'm so worried about him.

604
00:41:58,766 --> 00:42:02,645
He did things no one in this
White House should be proud of.

605
00:42:26,627 --> 00:42:27,834
Hey, Mom.

606
00:42:27,836 --> 00:42:29,795
How many people do you have there?

607
00:42:29,797 --> 00:42:31,296
Not many.

608
00:42:31,298 --> 00:42:33,175
Nobody that interesting, anyway.

609
00:42:34,468 --> 00:42:36,385
Her visit to the base.

610
00:42:36,387 --> 00:42:37,511
Independence speech,

611
00:42:37,513 --> 00:42:39,972
even the naughty soldier
is playing well for her.

612
00:42:39,974 --> 00:42:42,434
Her charmed life persists.

613
00:42:43,060 --> 00:42:45,018
Tell Uncle Bill we should step up.

614
00:42:45,020 --> 00:42:47,938
I can manage the narrative. De-charm her.

615
00:42:47,940 --> 00:42:49,265
He won't like that.

616
00:42:49,268 --> 00:42:51,777
He's not going to like any of
it, so what's the difference?

617
00:42:52,778 --> 00:42:55,948
- I don't know.
- Mom, just say the word.

618
00:42:58,242 --> 00:42:59,533
Trust me.

619
00:42:59,535 --> 00:43:02,523
Don't do a thing until
I get back to you.

620
00:43:22,725 --> 00:43:24,852
Can't even tell you're still in DC.

621
00:43:25,534 --> 00:43:28,537
There are parts of this city
that will always remain a secret.

622
00:43:33,256 --> 00:43:34,672
I don't think I'll be welcome back

623
00:43:34,675 --> 00:43:37,428
in the White House press
pool anytime soon, FYI.

624
00:43:38,324 --> 00:43:39,366
Doesn't matter.

625
00:43:41,535 --> 00:43:43,076
She won't speak to me.

626
00:43:43,078 --> 00:43:45,873
- An interview? Not after...
- She will.

627
00:43:54,562 --> 00:43:57,049
Tonight, for the first
time, he was forthcoming.

628
00:43:57,051 --> 00:43:58,884
But still saying he's guilty?

629
00:43:58,886 --> 00:44:00,346
Yeah, as always.

630
00:44:01,430 --> 00:44:04,014
And did he mention Mr. Grayson by name?

631
00:44:04,016 --> 00:44:06,685
No. He was just more distressed.

632
00:44:07,353 --> 00:44:08,727
So he's getting worse?

633
00:44:08,729 --> 00:44:10,606
Or better, he's finally talking.

634
00:44:12,752 --> 00:44:16,111
But the idea of his leaving
the facility anytime soon,

635
00:44:16,737 --> 00:44:20,280
that's still out of the question.
I mean, for his own good.

636
00:44:20,282 --> 00:44:21,843
He's either guilty of the crime

637
00:44:21,846 --> 00:44:24,309
- or psychotic for admitting to it.
- Madam President,

638
00:44:24,312 --> 00:44:26,411
Miss Shepherd is on
the White House line.

639
00:44:29,291 --> 00:44:32,584
Well, maybe it's time to give
him one of the antipsychotics.

640
00:44:32,586 --> 00:44:36,006
Possibly. But we'd have to
consider his history of addiction.

641
00:44:37,173 --> 00:44:39,548
By the way, in answer to your question,

642
00:44:39,551 --> 00:44:41,259
it was a letter opener,

643
00:44:41,261 --> 00:44:44,429
the last thing that Francis
gave him from his desk.

644
00:44:44,431 --> 00:44:46,264
Keep me posted, Charles.

645
00:44:46,266 --> 00:44:47,653
And thank you.

646
00:44:47,656 --> 00:44:49,144
Sure.

647
00:44:55,567 --> 00:44:57,945
Are you prepared?

648
00:44:59,780 --> 00:45:00,781
Excuse me.

649
00:45:01,490 --> 00:45:04,574
Don't you remember? Andover?

650
00:45:04,576 --> 00:45:06,076
Our prep school joke?

651
00:45:06,078 --> 00:45:10,290
I'm prepared to fail the test.

652
00:45:11,874 --> 00:45:13,127
Are you all right?

653
00:45:16,046 --> 00:45:20,426
I'm sorry to call so late. I...
I heard about what happened today.

654
00:45:23,345 --> 00:45:25,389
These things have a
tendency to leak out.

655
00:45:26,243 --> 00:45:27,474
I'm worried about you.

656
00:45:28,267 --> 00:45:30,767
It's bulletproof, the motorcade glass.

657
00:45:30,769 --> 00:45:32,187
Did they catch the guy?

658
00:45:33,439 --> 00:45:35,190
How do you know it's a guy?

659
00:45:36,150 --> 00:45:37,651
It's always a guy, right?

660
00:45:43,240 --> 00:45:47,369
What was it Bill wanted
adjusted in my speech?

661
00:45:50,122 --> 00:45:52,892
Oh. Something about...

662
00:45:52,895 --> 00:45:54,418
freedom.

663
00:45:58,756 --> 00:46:00,424
You're in my thoughts, Claire.

664
00:46:01,825 --> 00:46:03,093
Thank you.

665
00:46:04,136 --> 00:46:06,303
He's still with us, don't you think?

666
00:46:06,305 --> 00:46:07,555
Who?

667
00:46:07,556 --> 00:46:09,430
Frank.

668
00:46:09,433 --> 00:46:12,770
We're so glad we got to spend so
much time with him before he died.

669
00:46:15,189 --> 00:46:17,149
He told us some fascinating things.

670
00:46:19,359 --> 00:46:20,652
He's been on my mind.

671
00:46:24,490 --> 00:46:27,326
Bill and I finally went down
to Gaffney a few days ago...

672
00:46:30,120 --> 00:46:31,622
to pay our respects.

673
00:48:20,606 --> 00:48:21,938
Claire!

674
00:48:21,940 --> 00:48:23,440
I shouldn't have let them.

675
00:48:23,442 --> 00:48:24,831
I'm sorry.

676
00:48:24,834 --> 00:48:26,318
Come on out now.

677
00:48:31,909 --> 00:48:33,408
Are you still there?

678
00:48:33,410 --> 00:48:34,644
Yes.

679
00:48:44,421 --> 00:48:45,923
Help!

680
00:48:50,161 --> 00:48:52,074
Help!

681
00:49:08,195 --> 00:49:09,444
I know.

682
00:49:09,446 --> 00:49:11,573
You want to know what
really happened to him.

683
00:49:12,491 --> 00:49:14,743
A man like Francis doesn't just die.

684
00:49:15,452 --> 00:49:16,855
That would be...

685
00:49:16,858 --> 00:49:18,246
what's the word?

686
00:49:19,699 --> 00:49:21,152
"Convenient."

687
00:49:40,784 --> 00:49:50,789
